WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.480
In the early church, a Christian guilty
of a grave sin did penance in public.

2
00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.420
Usually it was allowed
only once in a lifetime,

3
00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.320
so many put it off until their deathbed.

4
00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:15.510
Then, in Ireland, monks began
writing small handbooks.

5
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:19.030
Each sin had its own fixed
penance: one for anger,

6
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.250
another for greed, another for envy.

7
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.240
So why did Irish monks write
a penance for every sin?

8
00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:29.130
In this mission, you'll open
three of these handbooks,

9
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.090
by Finnian, Columbanus and Cummean.

10
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.600
Then you'll ask what they tell historians,

11
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.940
and whether their ideas on
forgiveness still matter.

12
00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:42.680
Your mission: find out why Irish
monks wrote a penance for every sin.
